BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 605.3 Tm (interval?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 588.6 Tm (Answer: B. At the beginning of the QRS complex.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 572.3 Tm (Explanation: The PR interval measures from the onset of atrial depolarisation \(P-wave onset\) to the onset of) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 559.2 Tm (ventricular depolarisation \(QRS onset\).) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 543.1 Tm (Not A: The start of T occurs after ventricular depolarisation.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 529.7 Tm (Not C: Including all of QRS would extend beyond the PR interval.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 516.3 Tm (Not D: The end of T is used to mark the end of the QT interval.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 502.9 Tm (Source: lecture-7:Q19 | 6-7) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 479.7 Tm (2. Which response becomes particularly important for supporting pressure during sustained rather than) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 466.0 Tm (momentary upright posture?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 449.3 Tm (Answer: A. Renin-angiotensin effects and renal salt retention.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 433.0 Tm (Explanation: The rapid baroreflex stabilizes pressure within seconds, while the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 419.9 Tm (system supports longer-term pressure and volume.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 403.8 Tm (Not B: Aldosterone supports sodium retention rather than its suppression.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 390.4 Tm (Not C: Baroreflexes remain available during sustained standing.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 377.0 Tm (Not D: Loss of arteriolar responsiveness would worsen orthostatic control.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 363.6 Tm (Source: circulation-3:Q9 | Circulation 3, slide 4; Chapter 19 ?3) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 340.4 Tm (3. A person becomes angry during an argument, and both pulse and pressure rise. Which route can initiate this) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 326.7 Tm (response?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 310.0 Tm (Answer: A. Cortical signals acting through the hypothalamus on autonomic centres.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 293.7 Tm (Explanation: Emotions such as anger begin in the cortex. The cortex drives the hypothalamus, which excites the) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 280.6 Tm (vasomotor centre, raising sympathetic output so heart rate and blood pressure rise.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 264.5 Tm (Not B: Renal volume control is too slow and not the primary emotional route.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 251.1 Tm (Not C: The CSF pathways do not directly account for this autonomic response.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 237.7 Tm (Not D: Platelet serotonin is a local vascular constrictor, not the main emotional signal.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 224.3 Tm (Source: circulation-2:Q57 | 30, 33) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 201.1 Tm (4. Why can vision fail before consciousness during strong positive acceleration?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 184.4 Tm (Answer: A. Retinal perfusion falls as blood shifts toward the feet.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 168.1 Tm (Explanation: Positive g causes leg pooling and reduced pressure at eye level, so retinal perfusion can fail before) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 155.0 Tm (cerebral consciousness.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 138.9 Tm (Not B: Pressure at eye level decreases during positive g.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 125.5 Tm (Not C: The mechanism is reduced eye-level arterial perfusion, not an inevitable ICP excess.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 112.1 Tm (Not D: Headward blood movement occurs during negative g.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 98.7 Tm (Source: circulation-3:Q16 | Circulation 3, slide 9; Chapter 19 ?6) Tj ET
