BT /F2 12.0 Tf 1 1 1 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 815.0 Tm (DOMiNANCE / CVS PHYSIOLOGY) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 1 1 1 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 798.0 Tm (MOCK 03 | 40Q | EXPLANATION GUIDE | 60 MIN) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 745.0 Tm (20. A small additional deflection appears after the T wave in an otherwise interpretable trace. Which) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 731.3 Tm (description fits the U wave?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 714.6 Tm (Answer: B. A possible late ventricular repolarisation phenomenon.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 698.3 Tm (Explanation: The lecture describes a U wave, when seen after T, as the final remnants of ventricular repolarisation.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 685.2 Tm (Its precise physiological origin is uncertain.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 669.1 Tm (Not A: Atrial depolarisation is the P wave preceding QRS.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 655.7 Tm (Not C: Rapid ventricular depolarisation is represented by QRS, not U.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 642.3 Tm (Not D: The ventricular plateau is associated with the ST segment.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 628.9 Tm (Source: lecture-7:Q29 | 10) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 605.7 Tm (21. What is the main purpose of wide changes in skin blood flow during heating or cooling?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 589.0 Tm (Answer: C. To adjust body heat gain and loss.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 572.7 Tm (Explanation: Cutaneous vessels dilate to increase heat loss and constrict to conserve heat; flow can far exceed) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 559.6 Tm (nutritional needs.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 543.5 Tm (Not A: Skin metabolic demand is relatively modest compared with variation in perfusion.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 530.1 Tm (Not B: Heart pacemaking is not the main thermoregulatory role of skin circulation.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 516.7 Tm (Not D: Pulmonary-systemic pressure equality is unrelated to thermoregulation.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 503.3 Tm (Source: lecture-6:Q46 | 39-41) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 480.1 Tm (22. Why does sustained systemic hypertension tend to raise myocardial oxygen demand?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 463.4 Tm (Answer: D. The ventricle must generate higher pressure for ejection.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 447.1 Tm (Explanation: Higher arterial pressure increases ventricular pressure work and wall stress, requiring more oxygen) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 434.0 Tm (for force generation.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 417.9 Tm (Not A: Higher afterload generally increases wall stress.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 404.5 Tm (Not B: The myocardium continues to contract against the higher load.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 391.1 Tm (Not C: Coronary oxygen extraction remains substantial.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 377.7 Tm (Source: circulation-1:Q48 | 54; Chapter 8 ?7) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 354.5 Tm (23. Why is a distinct atrial repolarisation wave usually absent from a normal ECG?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 337.8 Tm (Answer: C. Its small electrical signal is obscured by the ventricular QRS complex.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 321.5 Tm (Explanation: Atrial repolarisation occurs around the time of ventricular depolarisation; the much larger QRS) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 308.4 Tm (electrical signal masks it on the surface trace.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 292.3 Tm (Not A: Repolarisation does generate voltage differences, although the signal is small.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 278.9 Tm (Not B: Atrial repolarisation does not need to await the beginning of ventricular diastole.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 265.5 Tm (Not D: That statement does not explain why the wave is not separately visible.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 252.1 Tm (Source: lecture-7:Q25 | Chapter 6 ?4 \(approved-chapter explanation\)) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 228.9 Tm (24. A measured QRS occupies 3.5 small squares at 25 mm/s. Which statement is supported by the lecture's usual) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 215.2 Tm (duration range?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 198.5 Tm (Answer: B. It lasts 0.14 s and is longer than the stated usual range.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 182.2 Tm (Explanation: At 25 mm/s, 3.5 small squares equal 3.5 x 0.04 = 0.14 s, above the lecture's usual QRS duration of) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 169.1 Tm (0.06-0.10 s.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 153.0 Tm (Not A: 0.10 s corresponds to 2.5 small squares at the standard speed.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 139.6 Tm (Not C: 0.07 s incorrectly halves the 0.14-s measured duration.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 126.2 Tm (Not D: 0.35 s does not follow from the standard 0.04-s small-square duration.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 112.8 Tm (Source: lecture-7:Q39 | 8, 13) Tj ET
