BT /F2 12.0 Tf 1 1 1 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 815.0 Tm (DOMiNANCE / CVS PHYSIOLOGY) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 1 1 1 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 798.0 Tm (MOCK 03 | 40Q | EXPLANATION GUIDE | 60 MIN) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 745.0 Tm (25. A patient becomes dizzy on standing after starting a sympatholytic drug. Which compensatory response is) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 731.3 Tm (most directly weakened?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 714.6 Tm (Answer: D. Constriction of systemic arterioles and veins.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 698.3 Tm (Explanation: Sympatholytic drugs blunt reflex arteriolar and venous constriction, so the circulation cannot) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 685.2 Tm (adequately oppose blood pooling and falling arterial pressure on standing.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 669.1 Tm (Not A: Walking uses a mechanical muscle pump, not this autonomic drug pathway.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 655.7 Tm (Not B: Venous valve directionality is mechanical and not directly abolished by sympatholytic drugs.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 642.3 Tm (Not C: Cerebral metabolic vasodilatation is not directly a sympathetic vasoconstrictor response.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 628.9 Tm (Source: circulation-3:Q15 | Circulation 3, slide 8; Chapter 19 ?5) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 605.7 Tm (26. A patient develops rapidly increasing intracranial pressure after a brain haemorrhage. What first) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 592.0 Tm (threatens cerebral perfusion?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 575.3 Tm (Answer: D. Compression of brain vessels and reduced driving pressure.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 559.0 Tm (Explanation: Rising intracranial pressure compresses intracranial vessels and lowers cerebral perfusion pressure,) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 545.9 Tm (risking medullary ischaemia.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 529.8 Tm (Not A: Raised intracranial pressure does not require systemic blood loss.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 516.4 Tm (Not B: Dilation cannot prevent the loss of cerebral driving pressure as intracranial pressure rises.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 503.0 Tm (Not C: Perfusion initially becomes harder as intracranial pressure rises.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 489.6 Tm (Source: circulation-2:Q53 | 31-32, 35-36) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 466.4 Tm (27. What closes the aortic valve at the beginning of ventricular relaxation?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 449.7 Tm (Answer: C. Aortic pressure exceeds ventricular pressure.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 433.4 Tm (Explanation: When ventricular pressure falls below aortic pressure, brief reversal tendency closes the aortic valve) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 420.3 Tm (and starts isovolumetric relaxation.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 404.2 Tm (Not A: Venous pressure does not directly determine aortic valve closure.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 390.8 Tm (Not B: Atrial pressure governs mitral opening, not aortic closure.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 377.4 Tm (Not D: Ventricular pressure exceeding aortic pressure opens the valve.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 364.0 Tm (Source: circulation-1:Q23 | 17) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 340.8 Tm (28. Which sequence best describes the classical triple response of Lewis?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 324.1 Tm (Answer: B. Red line, flare and wheal.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 307.8 Tm (Explanation: The classical triple response consists of local redness, a spreading flare and an oedematous wheal.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 291.7 Tm (Not A: A white line is a separate lighter-stroke reaction.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 278.3 Tm (Not C: Wheal formation requires local fluid leak rather than none.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 264.9 Tm (Not D: Thrombosis and scar are not normal features.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 251.5 Tm (Source: lecture-6:Q52 | 40) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 228.3 Tm (29. A patient has had high arterial pressure for months. Why might baroreceptors fail to drive pressure back) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 214.6 Tm (to the original baseline?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 197.9 Tm (Answer: A. Their operating range resets around the sustained level.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 181.6 Tm (Explanation: When pressure stays high for days, the baroreceptors reset and treat the new level as normal, so they) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 168.5 Tm (defend it instead of correcting it. Long-term pressure is set mainly by the kidneys' control of salt and water.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 152.4 Tm (Not B: Unbounded sensitivity would strongly oppose the persistent rise.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 139.0 Tm (Not C: Sensory fibres do not become motor fibres.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 125.6 Tm (Not D: Resetting does not require loss of every afferent signal.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 112.2 Tm (Source: circulation-2:Q44 | 18, 24-27) Tj ET
